    {
      "sectionId": "section-disclaimer",
      "heading": "Understanding RPGBOT’s Rating System",
      "sourceAnchor": "disclaimer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-disclaimer-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "RPGBOT uses a color coding scheme which is simple to understand and easy to read at a glance. This system is designed to help players make informed decisions for their dnd character builds."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-disclaimer-2",
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Red"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ": Bad, useless options, or options which\n\t\tare extremely situational. Nearly never useful."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Orange"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ": OK options, or useful options\n\t\tthat only apply in rare circumstances. Useful sometimes."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Green"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ": Good options. Useful often."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Blue"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ": Fantastic options, often essential\n\t\tto the function of your character. Useful very frequently."
              }
            ]
          ],
          "depths": [
            0,
            0,
            0,
            0
          ],
          "itemKinds": [
            "unordered",
            "unordered",
            "unordered",
            "unordered"
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-disclaimer-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The advice offered below is based on the current "
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "State of the 2014 DnD 5e Character Optimization Meta",
              "href": "https://rpgbot.net/dnd5/characters/state-of-the-meta/"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " as of when the article was last updated. Keep in mind that the state of the meta periodically changes as new source materials are released, and the article will be updated accordingly as time allows."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-disclaimer-4",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "We will not include 3rd-party content, Unearthed Arcnaa content, or homebrew in guides for official content because we can’t assume that your game will allow them."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Dwarf Classes (Customizable Origins)",
      "sourceAnchor": "classes-custom-origin",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classes-custom-origin-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "This section assumes that you’re using the option “"
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "Customizing Your Origin",
              "href": "https://www.dndbeyond.com/sources/tcoe/character-options#CustomizingYourOrigin"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "” rules presented in Tasha’s Cauldron of Everything. If you’re not using those rules, scroll down to the next section."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-artificer",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Artificer",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-artificer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-artificer-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Any variety of dwarf works fine for the Artificer, though the Mountain Dwarf’s armor proficiencies are wasted. Hill Dwarf is a great choice for front-line artificers because the additional hit points bring you more in line with martial classes like the Fighter, but Mark of Warding’s access to Armor of Agathys can solve the same problem."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-barbarian",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Barbarian",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-barbarian",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-barbarian-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Mountain Dwarf remains an excellent choice. The ability to rush two ability scores to 18 at level 4 gives you a significant mathematical advantage. The Duergar’s ability to cast Enlarge provides expanded reach and a nice damage bonus, but you can’t concentrate on it while raging."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-bard",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Bard",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-bard",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-bard-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "With the exception of College of Valor, bards are stuck in light armor.\n\tUnless you’re dead set on fighting with weapons, you likely won’t have the\n\tDexterity to make your AC passable. The Mountain Dwarf conveniently solves\n\tthat problem by giving you medium armor, thereby reducing your Dexterity needs\n\tto just 14."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-cleric",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Cleric",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-cleric",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-cleric-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Dwarfs continue to make fine clerics, but they don’t have any unique synergy\n\twith the Cleric."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-druid",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Druid",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-druid",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-druid-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Hill Dwarf’s additional hit points are wonderful for a class notorious\n\tfor being frail, but otherwise there’s little synergy here."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-fighter",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Fighter",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-fighter",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-fighter-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Mountain Dwarf’s two +2 increases are tempting because they leave so much room for feats, and the Hill Dwarf’s additional hit points are nice, but that’s about it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-fighter-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Duergar’s ability to cast Enlarge/Reduce also stacks with the Rune Knight’s Giant’s Might feature, allowing you to reach Gargantuan size. "
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-monk",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Monk",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-monk",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-monk-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "It’s honestly hard to choose between the Hill Dwarf and the Mountain Dwarf\n\tfor the Monk. The Hill Dwarf’s additional hit points are great since the Monk\n\thas d8 hit points and their AC scales very slowly, and reducing your need to\n\tincrease Constitution is really nice. But the Mountain Dwarf gets two +2\n\tincreases, which is really nice for the most MAD class in the game. Similar to\n\tthe Barbarian, you can start with 17 in Dexterity and Wisdom, then raise both\n\tto 18 at level 4, giving you an AC of 18 and setting you mathematically ahead\n\tof every other monk in the game for at least 8+ levels."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-paladin",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Paladin",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-paladin",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-paladin-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Mountain Dwarf’s two +2 increases are great on the Paladin. Much like the Barbarian, start with 17 in Strength and Charisma, then raise both to 18 at level 4. If dragonmarks are an option, consider the Mark of Warding for access to spells like Armor of Agathys. The Duergar’s ability to cast Enlarge can also provide a combat buff similar to Divine Favor."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-ranger",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Ranger",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-ranger",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-ranger-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Dwarf works for basically any ranger build. Mark of Warding offers access to Mage Armor, which gives Dexterity-based builds as much armor as a character in full plate. Mountain Dwarf offers two +2 increases, which is hard to argue against. Hill Dwarf’s bonus hit points will help offset the Ranger’s relatively poor durability compared to the Fighter. The Duergar’s access to Enlarge/Reduce and Invisibility offer some magical options that most rangers can’t replicate."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-rogue",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Rogue",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-rogue",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-rogue-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Rogue is a fine choice for the dwarf. Darkvision and extra durability are welcome, but the Rogue is very SAD compared to most martial classes so the Mountain Dwarf’s extra +2 feels wasted, and the Hill Dwarf’s extra hit points aren’t especially useful when you have things like Uncanny Dodge to pad your durability."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-rogue-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Duergar’s ability to cast Invisibility is nice, but it’s only once per day. Still, that might keep you happy until you can get enough Arcane Trickster spells to cast it again. Enlarge could be useful, too, but be cautious about turning yourself into a giant target."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-sorcerer",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Sorcerer",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-sorcerer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-sorcerer-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Mountain Dwarf. +2 Constitution, +2 Charisma, medium armor, and suddenly\n\tyou’re incredibly durable with almost no effort."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-warlock",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Warlock",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-warlock",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-warlock-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Mountain Dwarf. +2 Constitution, +2 Charisma, medium armor, and suddenly\n\tyou’re incredibly durable with almost no effort. That’s a bit of a waste on\n\tthe Hexblade, so consider the Hill Dwarf instead to help pad your d8 hit\n\tponts."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-custom-origin-wizard",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Wizard",
      "sourceAnchor": "custom-origin-wizard",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-custom-origin-wizard-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Mountain Dwarf. +2 Constitution, +2 Intelligence, medium armor, and suddenly\n\tyou’re incredibly durable with almost no effort."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Dwarf Classes (Default Rules)",
      "sourceAnchor": "classes-default",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classes-default-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "This section assumes that you’re "
            },
            {
              "type": "emphasis",
              "text": "not"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " using the option “Customizing Your Origin” rules presented in Tasha’s Cauldron of Everything or the updated version of the race published in Mordenkainen’s Monsters of the Multiverse. If you are using those rules, scroll up to the previous section."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-artificer",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Artificer",
      "sourceAnchor": "artificer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-artificer-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Mark of Warding provides the crucial Intelligence increase needed to make the\n\tArtificer work, and access to Armor of Agathys is a great improvement to the\n\tArtificer’s already impressive durability. The Dwarf’s natural durability\n\tmakes for a great front-line Artificer, so dwarf artificers should strongly\n\tconsider the Armorer or the Battlesmith. Other dwarf subraces lack the crucial\n\tIntelligence increase."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-barbarian",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Barbarian",
      "sourceAnchor": "barbarian",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-barbarian-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Mountain Dwarf gets +2 to both Strength and Constitution, making it a\n\tspectacular option for the Barbarian. If you can start with 15 Str, 14 Dex,\n\tand 15 Con as your base ability scores you’re off to a great start and your\n\t4th-level ability increase will be extremely impactful if you split it between\n\tStrength and Constitution to get both to 18. Fast Movement will correct the\n\tDwarf’s relatively poor speed, and the Dwarf’s other defensive racial traits\n\twill add to the Barbarian’s already impressive durability."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-bard",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Bard",
      "sourceAnchor": "bard",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-bard-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Dwarf doesn’t get anything that complements the bard’s skillset. Mountain\n\tDwarf offers medium armor proficiency, which is tempting for most bard\n\tsubclasses, but that’s not enough to make the Dwarf a good option."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-cleric",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Cleric",
      "sourceAnchor": "cleric",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-cleric-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Dwarf’s natural durability is a great option for clerics since clerics\n\toften do double-duty as a healer and a defender. The Hill Dwarf offers a\n\tcrucial Wisdom increase, making them the best subrace for the Cleric."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-druid",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Druid",
      "sourceAnchor": "druid",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-druid-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Hill Dwarf offers a Constitution increase, a Wisdom increase, and extra\n\thit points on top of all of that. Those are great options for Druids, who have\n\tterrible AC and only 1d8 hit points, but still find themselves in melee more\n\toften than they probably should. The Dwarf’s poison resilience will be\n\tsomewhat redundant, but that shouldn’t deter you."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-fighter",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Fighter",
      "sourceAnchor": "fighter",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-fighter-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Every subrace works for the Fighter, but the Duergar and the Mountain Dwarf are the go-to options for their Strength increases. "
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-fighter-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Duergar’s ability to cast Enlarge/Reduce also stacks with the Rune Knight’s Giant’s Might feature, allowing you to reach Gargantuan size."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-monk",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Monk",
      "sourceAnchor": "monk",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-monk-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "A Hill Dwarf monk is much more durable than most monks, but without a\n\tDexterity increase you’ll be weak offensively, and you won’t hit 20 Dexterity\n\tfor a long time."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-paladin",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Paladin",
      "sourceAnchor": "paladin",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-paladin-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Mountain Dwarf makes a fine Paladin, and the dwarf’s natural durability\n\tnicely complements the Paladin’s already exceptional durability."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-ranger",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Ranger",
      "sourceAnchor": "ranger",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-ranger-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Hill Dwarf is an excellent choice for a Druidic Warrior build. You can\n\tessentially build yourself like a druid (Wis, Con, and a splash of Dex) and be\n\tjust as effective in melee as the best Strength-based ranger builds. The Hill\n\tDwarf’s additional hit points will help offset your relatively poor AC\n\tcompared to martial classes like the Fighter and the Paladin."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-ranger-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Mountain Dwarf could work for a Strength-based build, but mechanically\n\tit’s just not a good option. Too much of the Mountain Dwarf’s value is tied up\n\tin their armor proficiencies, and the Ranger already gets them. In a\n\tsubterranean campaign, the Duergar could make an interesting ranger, but it’s\n\tnot going to be amazingly effective."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-rogue",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Rogue",
      "sourceAnchor": "rogue",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-rogue-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Darkvision is great, but that’s the best thing that the Dwarf can offer the\n\tRogue. A Dexterity increase is simply too important."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-sorcerer",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Sorcerer",
      "sourceAnchor": "sorcerer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-sorcerer-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "No Charisma increase."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-warlock",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Warlock",
      "sourceAnchor": "warlock",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-warlock-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "No Charisma increase."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-wizard",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Wizard",
      "sourceAnchor": "wizard",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-wizard-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Mark of Warding provides an Intelligence increase, and the Dwarf’s core\n\tracial traits provide durability that the Wizard sorely needs. If you’re\n\twilling to forgo an Intelligence increase, the Mountain Dwarf’s armor\n\tproficiencies will provide a significant boost to your AC."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-feats",
      "heading": "Dwarf Feats",
      "sourceAnchor": "feats",
      "blocks": []
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-dwarven-fortitude",
      "parentSectionId": "section-feats",
      "heading": "Dwarven FortitudeXGtE",
      "sourceAnchor": "dwarven-fortitude",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-dwarven-fortitude-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The ability to heal in combat is neat, but this feat decreases in value as\n\tyou gain levels. 1d12+5 is basically nothing at 20th level. The only real\n\texception I see is the Monk’s Patient Defense, which allows you to do this as\n\ta bonus action. But even then, this isn’t impressive, and with the\n\tintroduction of Way of Mercy the Monk can already accomplish a similar gimick\n\twithout the expense of a feat."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-squat-nimbleness",
      "parentSectionId": "section-feats",
      "heading": "Squat NimblenessXGtE",
      "sourceAnchor": "squat-nimbleness",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-squat-nimbleness-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Interesting, but I don’t know if I could find a way to squeeze it into a\n\tbuild. Maybe a dwarf ranger or rogue could take it while raising their\n\tDexterity from 19 to 20, but that seems unlikely."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-dragonmarks",
      "heading": "Dwarf Dragonmarks",
      "sourceAnchor": "dragonmarks",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-dragonmarks-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Dragonmarks are detailed in Eberron: Rising from the Last War. Dwarves treat\n\tdragonmarks like a subrace, retaining their core racial traits and adding the\n\ttraits provided by the dragonmark."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-mark-of-warding",
      "parentSectionId": "section-dragonmarks",
      "heading": "Mark of Warding",
      "sourceAnchor": "mark-of-warding",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-mark-of-warding-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Constitution and Intelligence are an interesting combination. Mark of\n\tWarding’s ability increases work best for arcane trickster rogues, artificers,\n\teldritch knight fighters, and wizards. The extra Constitution can be very\n\thelpful for spellcasters who frequently find themselves drawing fire like\n\tfront-line artificers or eldritch knights.\n"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-mark-of-warding-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The peculiar combination of skill bonuses and innate spells fits the theme of\n\tthe Dragonmark well, but it’s not always useful for spellcasters who are\n\tsetting out for a life of adventure. Alarm can be useful while resting\n\tsomewhere dangerous, but mage armor and similar options (including real armor)\n\tare easily available to any class which could reasonably benefit from the Mark\n\tof Warding’s ability increases. The spell list also overlaps heavily with\n\tspells already available to spellcasting classes which depend on intelligence,\n\tthough it notably includes Armor of Agathys to tempt melee spellcasters."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
